1
00:00:48,006 --> 00:00:54,971
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

2
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:57,974
EPISODE 10

3
00:00:58,641 --> 00:01:01,603
His Excellency is here!

4
00:01:27,504 --> 00:01:32,008
Today, May 15th, 1932,

5
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:34,594
Joseon's first pneumatic tube system

6
00:01:34,677 --> 00:01:37,388
has successfully been unveiled!

7
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:47,899
The Great Japanese Empire's efforts
for the development of Joseon

8
00:01:47,982 --> 00:01:52,237
have finally borne fruit today.

9
00:01:53,279 --> 00:01:54,239
This marks

10
00:01:54,948 --> 00:01:59,202
a new milestone in Joseon's modernization

11
00:01:59,786 --> 00:02:03,623
and the first step
toward Manseon's prosperity…

12
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:06,000
MANSEON
MANCHURIA AND JOSEON

13
00:02:06,084 --> 00:02:08,169
…eventually reaching Manchukuo!

14
00:02:32,485 --> 00:02:34,237
Interpreter Sato!

15
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:37,448
Interpreter Sato, over here.

16
00:02:46,958 --> 00:02:49,627
Interpret my speech into English.

17
00:02:50,587 --> 00:02:51,588
Yes, sir.

18
00:03:42,138 --> 00:03:43,431
Interpreter Sato.

19
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,977
Why don't you join us for a toast?

20
00:03:52,815 --> 00:03:53,650
Yes, sir.

21
00:04:08,790 --> 00:04:10,083
Raise your glasses.

22
00:04:19,717 --> 00:04:22,220
<i>It'll go off at 6:20 p.m.</i>
<i>Get as far away as you can.</i>

23
00:04:26,307 --> 00:04:27,809
Cheers!

24
00:04:27,892 --> 00:04:29,102
- Cheers!
- Cheers!

25
00:04:37,026 --> 00:04:37,986
What's going on?

26
00:04:44,534 --> 00:04:45,368
Your Excellency.

27
00:04:45,451 --> 00:04:46,286
Yes?

28
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:52,041
What did you say?

29
00:04:53,084 --> 00:04:54,127
Excuse me, everyone.

30
00:05:14,022 --> 00:05:15,857
Due to an emergency,

31
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:18,234
the banquet has been canceled.

32
00:05:19,444 --> 00:05:23,406
We'll update you on the situation
once we've sorted things out.

33
00:05:23,489 --> 00:05:25,116
Please travel home safely.

34
00:05:26,075 --> 00:05:27,160
We're very sorry.

35
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:31,539
Chief of Police.

36
00:05:34,876 --> 00:05:37,003
Check security,
come to the Governor-General's Office.

37
00:05:37,670 --> 00:05:38,504
Yes, sir!

38
00:05:42,508 --> 00:05:43,634
Just now,

39
00:05:43,718 --> 00:05:45,928
Prime Minister Inukai
was attacked in Tokyo.

40
00:05:46,012 --> 00:05:48,890
MAY 15TH, 1932, 5:30 P.M.
SOLDIERS ATTACKED PRIME MINISTER INUKAI

41
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:53,144
We'd better leave.

42
00:06:06,491 --> 00:06:07,950
Please explain to us.

43
00:06:32,892 --> 00:06:34,394
- Fourth floor.
- Yes, sir.

44
00:06:49,951 --> 00:06:51,369
Interpreter Sato!

45
00:06:53,371 --> 00:06:56,249
Stop apologizing
and explain what's going on.

46
00:06:56,332 --> 00:06:57,583
Please return home today.

47
00:06:58,251 --> 00:07:00,420
- Interpreter Sato!
- Today's banquet is over.

48
00:07:00,503 --> 00:07:01,671
Please head home.

49
00:07:01,754 --> 00:07:03,047
Excuse me.

50
00:07:03,631 --> 00:07:04,507
Mr. Sato!

51
00:07:06,926 --> 00:07:07,760
Ms. Keum.

52
00:07:09,262 --> 00:07:10,471
Mr. Sato.

53
00:08:24,462 --> 00:08:26,631
I'm sorry. Please head home.

54
00:09:06,379 --> 00:09:08,422
Are you okay, Ms. Keum?

55
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:10,466
Now, let's get out.

56
00:09:20,726 --> 00:09:21,811
Was that a bomb?

57
00:10:18,743 --> 00:10:21,329
Someone get a stretcher!

58
00:10:36,177 --> 00:10:38,262
Mr. Sato.

59
00:10:41,974 --> 00:10:42,892
Mr. Sato!

60
00:10:45,561 --> 00:10:47,396
There might be another bomb!

61
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:49,815
Everyone, evacuate immediately!

62
00:10:59,909 --> 00:11:01,452
Mr. Sato!

63
00:11:03,996 --> 00:11:04,830
Mr. Sato.

64
00:11:10,836 --> 00:11:11,671
Ms. Keum.

65
00:11:13,714 --> 00:11:15,132
Are you all right?

66
00:11:15,216 --> 00:11:17,343
Yes, I'm fine.

67
00:11:17,426 --> 00:11:18,427
Are you okay?

68
00:11:18,511 --> 00:11:19,512
Yes.

69
00:11:22,848 --> 00:11:24,308
You need to get out of here.

70
00:11:25,059 --> 00:11:25,976
Someone, please…

71
00:11:26,894 --> 00:11:28,062
Somebody,

72
00:11:28,562 --> 00:11:30,147
help this person!

73
00:11:30,856 --> 00:11:31,941
Get outside, now.

74
00:11:38,739 --> 00:11:39,573
Ms. Keum.

75
00:11:39,657 --> 00:11:40,741
Mr. Sato!

76
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:42,410
Are you okay?

77
00:11:51,711 --> 00:11:54,046
Madam!

78
00:11:55,005 --> 00:11:55,965
Madam!

79
00:11:56,757 --> 00:11:58,342
What are you doing here? Let's get out.

80
00:11:58,426 --> 00:11:59,427
What?

81
00:12:00,386 --> 00:12:01,971
We'll all get caught if we don't hurry.

82
00:12:02,054 --> 00:12:04,265
You're leaving for America anyway.
Why act loyal?

83
00:12:05,474 --> 00:12:06,308
Move. Let's go.

84
00:12:06,392 --> 00:12:07,518
Myung-nam, hurry.

85
00:12:07,601 --> 00:12:10,062
It's okay, Dad. We're almost there.

86
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:25,161
Ms. Keum!

87
00:12:26,203 --> 00:12:27,037
Ms. Keum…

88
00:12:32,042 --> 00:12:33,085
Ms. Keum!

89
00:12:47,016 --> 00:12:48,642
Who's behind all this?

90
00:12:57,234 --> 00:12:59,779
Was that explosion your doing too?

91
00:13:06,786 --> 00:13:09,830
Tell me. Are you with the Gugukdan too?

92
00:13:16,504 --> 00:13:17,338
Independence…

93
00:13:18,923 --> 00:13:19,757
for…

94
00:13:23,552 --> 00:13:24,637
Korea.

95
00:13:37,024 --> 00:13:37,983
Father?

96
00:13:42,488 --> 00:13:43,489
He's an independence fighter.

97
00:13:59,129 --> 00:13:59,964
Shoot.

98
00:14:01,966 --> 00:14:03,008
Kill him.

99
00:15:17,458 --> 00:15:18,500
We have to run.

100
00:15:18,584 --> 00:15:20,669
The police will be here soon
after those gunshots.

101
00:15:22,421 --> 00:15:23,756
Inspector General!

102
00:15:27,843 --> 00:15:28,761
Inspector General!

103
00:15:30,721 --> 00:15:32,097
I just heard the gunshots.

104
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:36,894
A bomb went off in the banquet hall.

105
00:15:36,977 --> 00:15:40,105
There may be more bombs.
We need to get outside.

106
00:15:56,789 --> 00:15:58,207
Songjeon.

107
00:15:58,749 --> 00:15:59,875
Songjeon.

108
00:16:00,626 --> 00:16:01,794
Songjeon!

109
00:16:03,545 --> 00:16:04,380
Songjeon…

110
00:16:05,255 --> 00:16:06,465
So-ran.

111
00:16:18,018 --> 00:16:18,936
So-ran, we have to go.

112
00:16:20,145 --> 00:16:21,313
Songjeon.

113
00:16:22,064 --> 00:16:24,191
Wake up, Songjeon!

114
00:16:26,235 --> 00:16:27,069
So-ran.

115
00:17:12,781 --> 00:17:13,949
Get in, Dad.

116
00:17:16,744 --> 00:17:17,619
Hold on.

117
00:17:21,373 --> 00:17:23,500
What are you doing?
Go to Gyeongseong Station already.

118
00:17:24,209 --> 00:17:25,711
What about Mrs. Yoo?

119
00:17:26,628 --> 00:17:28,047
Did you not hear me earlier?

120
00:17:28,130 --> 00:17:29,089
You should go already.

121
00:17:29,173 --> 00:17:30,299
Aren't you going to America?

122
00:17:30,382 --> 00:17:31,467
Myung-nam!

123
00:17:31,967 --> 00:17:33,093
Get over here!

124
00:17:33,594 --> 00:17:36,472
Myung-nam, hurry!

125
00:17:36,555 --> 00:17:38,390
Myung-nam, get in here.

126
00:18:22,851 --> 00:18:23,685
This way!

127
00:18:26,772 --> 00:18:28,315
The suspect went that way!

128
00:18:28,398 --> 00:18:29,233
Yes, sir!

129
00:18:50,462 --> 00:18:51,380
A woman?

130
00:18:58,220 --> 00:18:59,304
How did it go?

131
00:18:59,930 --> 00:19:02,182
Did Songjeon make it out?

132
00:19:04,101 --> 00:19:05,519
I'm not sure.

133
00:19:12,234 --> 00:19:14,111
All the papers for America
are in that bag.

134
00:19:15,904 --> 00:19:18,907
So-ran packed this for you
to eat on the ship.

135
00:19:23,829 --> 00:19:26,915
Wipe your face first
or you'll get caught at a security check.

136
00:19:41,722 --> 00:19:42,681
Hang in there.

137
00:19:52,983 --> 00:19:53,817
Mr. Hideo.

138
00:20:09,708 --> 00:20:11,043
Let's get going.

139
00:20:40,864 --> 00:20:41,740
<i>Shoot.</i>

140
00:20:43,951 --> 00:20:44,785
Hideo!

141
00:21:19,653 --> 00:21:21,154
I'm done going easy on you.

142
00:21:22,823 --> 00:21:23,782
Don't forget.

143
00:21:24,283 --> 00:21:27,286
You're nobody if I'm dead.

144
00:23:19,564 --> 00:23:25,987
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

145
00:23:34,538 --> 00:23:36,832
The Foreign Affairs chief
died on the spot.

146
00:23:36,915 --> 00:23:38,750
The head of General Affairs
at the Communications Bureau

147
00:23:38,834 --> 00:23:40,794
died on the way to the hospital.

148
00:23:42,421 --> 00:23:43,296
I see.

149
00:23:44,089 --> 00:23:47,801
I wonder what you'll have to say
for yourself this time, Inspector General,

150
00:23:48,593 --> 00:23:50,512
with that big mouth of yours.

151
00:23:50,595 --> 00:23:53,515
Didn't you preach about
using the situation to our advantage?

152
00:23:55,559 --> 00:24:00,147
You swore you'd round up every last one
of those subversives.

153
00:24:01,022 --> 00:24:02,941
A bomb, here in the Government-General?

154
00:24:04,734 --> 00:24:05,944
How dare you

155
00:24:06,027 --> 00:24:09,281
make a fool of me!

156
00:24:10,907 --> 00:24:12,284
I take full responsibility.

157
00:24:12,367 --> 00:24:13,452
I'm sorry.

158
00:24:13,535 --> 00:24:14,828
You incompetent fool!

159
00:24:16,037 --> 00:24:18,957
A colonial resident like you never
deserved the position in the first place.

160
00:24:19,040 --> 00:24:20,459
Step down now!

161
00:24:24,087 --> 00:24:26,006
Will that solve everything?

162
00:24:27,549 --> 00:24:28,508
What?

163
00:24:32,012 --> 00:24:33,638
If I step down,

164
00:24:34,848 --> 00:24:37,809
will everyone forget the bomb went off
at the Government-General?

165
00:24:37,893 --> 00:24:38,935
Inspector General!

166
00:24:39,019 --> 00:24:42,147
Yun Bong-gil's bombing in Shanghai
was only two weeks ago.

167
00:24:43,023 --> 00:24:45,859
The League of Nations team
arrives in Joseon in under two months.

168
00:24:45,942 --> 00:24:46,985
At a time like this,

169
00:24:47,736 --> 00:24:50,864
a bomb goes off
at the Government-General of Joseon

170
00:24:50,947 --> 00:24:52,657
and the inspector general resigns…

171
00:24:53,867 --> 00:24:56,411
What will the international community
make of that?

172
00:24:57,370 --> 00:25:01,249
The whole thing happened
in front of the foreign press.

173
00:25:01,333 --> 00:25:03,543
How are you supposed to bury that?

174
00:25:03,627 --> 00:25:04,669
Today's incident

175
00:25:05,879 --> 00:25:09,257
was a random act by a Japanese man
who's mentally unstable.

176
00:25:10,383 --> 00:25:14,429
No independence fighters
ever infiltrated the Government-General.

177
00:25:15,096 --> 00:25:16,181
This is a time

178
00:25:17,057 --> 00:25:19,351
when Joseon must appear peaceful.

179
00:25:21,102 --> 00:25:21,978
Your Excellency.

180
00:25:24,606 --> 00:25:26,525
It went just as we planned.

181
00:25:27,943 --> 00:25:29,569
The outrage fades quickly.

182
00:25:29,653 --> 00:25:32,280
It's cleaning up the mess that matters.

183
00:25:33,532 --> 00:25:35,951
When someone offers
to wield the sword for us,

184
00:25:36,034 --> 00:25:37,702
there's no reason to say no.

185
00:25:40,038 --> 00:25:40,872
Yes, sir.

186
00:25:40,956 --> 00:25:43,833
I'll make sure
we catch the culprit tonight.

187
00:26:14,322 --> 00:26:15,740
I'm sorry.

188
00:26:16,283 --> 00:26:17,367
I'm sorry.

189
00:26:18,243 --> 00:26:19,619
This pawn ticket.

190
00:26:20,245 --> 00:26:21,913
Why'd you put it here?

191
00:26:22,872 --> 00:26:24,207
Do you not want

192
00:26:24,291 --> 00:26:26,751
anything to do
with someone like me anymore?

193
00:27:29,022 --> 00:27:29,939
<i>Ga-kyung.</i>

194
00:27:30,607 --> 00:27:34,069
<i>When you get to America,</i>
<i>live life your way.</i>

195
00:27:35,612 --> 00:27:38,281
<i>Neither I nor my daughter could do that.</i>

196
00:27:38,907 --> 00:27:42,077
<i>But I want you to have that chance,</i>
<i>no matter what.</i>

197
00:27:43,787 --> 00:27:45,163
<i>Be happy.</i>

198
00:27:46,373 --> 00:27:47,582
<i>You have to.</i>

199
00:27:54,714 --> 00:27:57,759
CASTELLA

200
00:28:01,262 --> 00:28:02,889
<i>Just as he was leaving,</i>

201
00:28:03,598 --> 00:28:05,016
<i>he said to me…</i>

202
00:28:06,476 --> 00:28:07,602
"When spring comes,

203
00:28:09,187 --> 00:28:11,147
let's take Seo-hee to Gyeongseong

204
00:28:12,107 --> 00:28:13,942
<i>and have some castella."</i>

205
00:28:19,572 --> 00:28:20,573
Incheon!

206
00:28:20,657 --> 00:28:24,244
The train for Incheon is about to depart.

207
00:28:58,319 --> 00:28:59,154
Watch your step.

208
00:29:03,283 --> 00:29:05,493
I'm not sure it was a good idea
to come home.

209
00:29:06,119 --> 00:29:08,079
You should get more treatment and rest.

210
00:29:08,163 --> 00:29:10,373
They'll get suspicious
if our house is empty.

211
00:29:11,458 --> 00:29:12,500
I can manage.

212
00:29:13,460 --> 00:29:14,294
All right.

213
00:29:15,920 --> 00:29:17,589
But don't push yourself too hard.

214
00:29:29,934 --> 00:29:31,227
Who is it?

215
00:29:31,811 --> 00:29:33,563
We're from the Government-General.

216
00:29:44,824 --> 00:29:46,242
What's going on?

217
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:55,001
Mr. Hideo.

218
00:30:10,683 --> 00:30:11,518
So-ran.

219
00:30:12,101 --> 00:30:14,896
The Government-General sent
the police with Mr. Hideo.

220
00:30:26,741 --> 00:30:27,909
Mr. Hideo.

221
00:30:27,992 --> 00:30:28,993
Hello.

222
00:30:29,786 --> 00:30:31,204
Sorry to bother you so late.

223
00:30:31,913 --> 00:30:33,081
We're checking everyone

224
00:30:33,164 --> 00:30:35,667
who may have been hurt
at the banquet today.

225
00:30:35,750 --> 00:30:38,419
Did you… not get hurt?

226
00:30:40,713 --> 00:30:42,215
Honey, what's all this about?

227
00:30:44,008 --> 00:30:44,884
Mr. Hideo.

228
00:30:44,968 --> 00:30:47,512
I appreciate you checking on us,
but it's awfully late--

229
00:30:47,595 --> 00:30:48,596
Wait.

230
00:30:49,597 --> 00:30:51,349
You're missing one earring.

231
00:30:52,183 --> 00:30:53,351
A pearl earring, at that.

232
00:30:58,690 --> 00:30:59,858
Put those guns away.

233
00:31:03,194 --> 00:31:04,404
Have you lost your mind?

234
00:31:04,487 --> 00:31:05,947
Stay back.

235
00:31:10,076 --> 00:31:11,244
I put the other earring

236
00:31:12,203 --> 00:31:14,122
in here.

237
00:31:14,205 --> 00:31:15,582
Stand back.

238
00:31:19,127 --> 00:31:20,461
I'll check.

239
00:31:27,260 --> 00:31:28,094
No.

240
00:31:30,847 --> 00:31:32,307
I'll check it.

241
00:31:34,517 --> 00:31:35,727
Put the guns down and step back.

242
00:32:01,461 --> 00:32:02,295
Madam!

243
00:32:03,546 --> 00:32:04,672
I'm back.

244
00:32:06,758 --> 00:32:07,842
Are you upstairs?

245
00:32:13,640 --> 00:32:15,224
I'm back!

246
00:32:15,308 --> 00:32:17,060
- Madam!
- Freeze!

247
00:32:29,405 --> 00:32:30,823
Mr. Sato.

248
00:32:31,532 --> 00:32:34,577
<i>Are you here to arrest us?</i>

249
00:32:35,620 --> 00:32:37,038
Put the guns down.

250
00:32:38,790 --> 00:32:39,624
Put them down.

251
00:32:43,878 --> 00:32:45,463
I'm Keum Seo-hee,
an English interpreter trainee

252
00:32:45,546 --> 00:32:48,257
at the Government-General,
and I live here with them.

253
00:32:49,092 --> 00:32:54,847
I work with Interpreter Sato Hideo here
at the Government-General.

254
00:32:59,644 --> 00:33:00,895
Are you okay, Madam?

255
00:33:02,438 --> 00:33:03,523
You look shaken up--

256
00:33:03,606 --> 00:33:04,774
Back off!

257
00:33:07,276 --> 00:33:08,111
Okay.

258
00:33:08,194 --> 00:33:09,153
Mr. Sato.

259
00:33:09,237 --> 00:33:12,031
Please check the drawer
for the other earring.

260
00:33:25,670 --> 00:33:26,546
Mr. Sato.

261
00:33:27,380 --> 00:33:28,756
Under your foot…

262
00:33:40,768 --> 00:33:41,728
Oh my.

263
00:33:41,811 --> 00:33:43,980
Why does this keep falling out?

264
00:33:44,063 --> 00:33:46,232
It almost got crushed under your foot.

265
00:33:47,442 --> 00:33:49,777
I'll go get this fixed tomorrow.

266
00:33:55,450 --> 00:33:57,035
Is that all you needed?

267
00:33:59,370 --> 00:34:02,498
You said you were checking on people
who may have been hurt from the bombing.

268
00:34:03,082 --> 00:34:05,001
Why are you pointing guns at us?

269
00:34:05,626 --> 00:34:07,712
The bombing at the Government-General?

270
00:34:09,213 --> 00:34:13,426
Wait, are you here to find the bomber?

271
00:34:14,093 --> 00:34:15,470
We can't comment on that.

272
00:34:15,553 --> 00:34:18,222
We need to check for shoulder injury.

273
00:34:18,306 --> 00:34:21,059
Both ladies who attended today's banquet.

274
00:34:21,142 --> 00:34:23,186
Please pull down your tops
and show your shoulders.

275
00:34:29,192 --> 00:34:31,861
How dare you make
such a demand of my wife?

276
00:34:31,944 --> 00:34:33,071
Step back, please.

277
00:34:38,326 --> 00:34:39,160
Shoulders?

278
00:34:40,078 --> 00:34:41,704
Just a moment.

279
00:34:44,290 --> 00:34:45,124
Like this?

280
00:34:46,542 --> 00:34:47,585
Will this do?

281
00:34:50,630 --> 00:34:52,173
I'm clear, right?

282
00:34:55,676 --> 00:34:57,303
Yes, you're clear.

283
00:34:59,347 --> 00:35:03,184
Now let me check the madam.

284
00:35:03,267 --> 00:35:04,102
That's enough.

285
00:35:10,942 --> 00:35:11,943
Let's get going.

286
00:35:12,026 --> 00:35:12,985
No, sir.

287
00:35:13,528 --> 00:35:14,695
We still need to check.

288
00:35:16,239 --> 00:35:17,448
Sorry about the trouble.

289
00:35:27,291 --> 00:35:28,126
- Excuse us.
- Excuse us.

290
00:35:52,316 --> 00:35:53,651
Sorry to have caused you trouble.

291
00:35:54,235 --> 00:35:55,194
We'll be in touch.

292
00:35:55,862 --> 00:35:56,696
Okay.

293
00:36:23,848 --> 00:36:25,266
They're gone.

294
00:36:27,310 --> 00:36:29,020
Madam! Are you okay?

295
00:36:29,979 --> 00:36:30,813
You're bleeding.

296
00:36:31,689 --> 00:36:33,024
Why are you back?

297
00:36:33,107 --> 00:36:33,941
What?

298
00:36:34,025 --> 00:36:36,736
You were supposed to be on the train.
Why'd you come back?

299
00:36:36,819 --> 00:36:39,155
That's not important right now. Wait--

300
00:36:39,238 --> 00:36:41,115
Going to America was your dream.

301
00:36:41,199 --> 00:36:42,783
So why would you come back?

302
00:36:46,996 --> 00:36:48,664
I can't stand owing anyone anything.

303
00:36:49,498 --> 00:36:50,333
What?

304
00:36:50,416 --> 00:36:52,877
You gave me this thick envelope,
so I opened it.

305
00:36:53,419 --> 00:36:55,922
It was the three months' pay
you'd originally promised me.

306
00:36:56,923 --> 00:36:58,883
I said I'd quit after just one month.

307
00:36:58,966 --> 00:37:00,468
There's no taking it back, okay?

308
00:37:00,551 --> 00:37:02,386
I can't give back
what you've already paid me.

309
00:37:03,554 --> 00:37:06,057
So I'll finish the full three months.

310
00:37:07,350 --> 00:37:08,601
Send me to America after that.

311
00:37:09,977 --> 00:37:12,897
If I took the money and bailed,
I'd be a crook,

312
00:37:13,689 --> 00:37:15,233
not a spy.

313
00:37:18,361 --> 00:37:19,195
So-ran.

314
00:37:28,079 --> 00:37:29,288
How did you find out?

315
00:37:33,125 --> 00:37:34,085
The thing is…

316
00:37:54,855 --> 00:37:56,440
We'd like your cooperation.

317
00:38:21,007 --> 00:38:22,967
Why are you headed to Incheon?

318
00:38:26,679 --> 00:38:29,223
I'm going to meet an American missionary.

319
00:38:29,307 --> 00:38:31,267
I've agreed to look after their baby.

320
00:38:43,195 --> 00:38:44,071
Raise your arms like this.

321
00:38:49,327 --> 00:38:50,161
Do this with your arms.

322
00:38:57,835 --> 00:38:59,170
I don't think she's been hurt.

323
00:39:03,299 --> 00:39:04,842
Over there, pearl earrings…

324
00:39:06,927 --> 00:39:08,554
See if she has both earrings.

325
00:39:08,637 --> 00:39:09,472
Yes, sir.

326
00:39:12,641 --> 00:39:13,768
<i>Pearl earrings?</i>

327
00:39:16,771 --> 00:39:17,605
She has both.

328
00:39:26,280 --> 00:39:27,323
Thank you.

329
00:39:31,535 --> 00:39:33,496
We appreciate your cooperation.

330
00:40:06,070 --> 00:40:07,905
Well, that's what happened.

331
00:40:13,369 --> 00:40:14,203
I see.

332
00:40:17,998 --> 00:40:19,625
So where'd you get the earring?

333
00:40:20,668 --> 00:40:21,544
The earring?

334
00:40:21,627 --> 00:40:25,631
THE FIRST JONGNO PAWNSHOP

335
00:40:27,174 --> 00:40:28,008
Mister!

336
00:40:28,092 --> 00:40:30,553
Mister, open the door!

337
00:40:30,636 --> 00:40:31,887
Wake up!

338
00:40:31,971 --> 00:40:33,097
Mister!

339
00:40:33,722 --> 00:40:35,599
- Mister.
- Who is it?

340
00:40:35,683 --> 00:40:38,352
- It's me, Ga-kyung.
- Who is it?

341
00:40:38,436 --> 00:40:39,854
Mister!

342
00:40:39,937 --> 00:40:41,105
Hurry up and open the door.

343
00:40:41,188 --> 00:40:42,982
Don't you know what time it is?

344
00:40:43,065 --> 00:40:43,899
Come on!

345
00:40:43,983 --> 00:40:47,653
Apparently, it's all the rage
among stylish ladies these days.

346
00:40:48,279 --> 00:40:52,575
I hear lots of men buy them as gifts
for their girlfriends.

347
00:40:58,539 --> 00:41:00,249
I promised to give it back
to the pawnshop tomorrow.

348
00:41:02,793 --> 00:41:06,464
I never expected Sato Hideo
to show up with the police.

349
00:41:06,547 --> 00:41:08,716
He's clearly suspicious of us.

350
00:41:09,675 --> 00:41:10,509
Even so,

351
00:41:11,427 --> 00:41:14,763
do you really think he came
because he suspects us?

352
00:41:16,432 --> 00:41:21,437
The police tried to check her shoulder,
but he stopped them, so they just left.

353
00:41:22,646 --> 00:41:24,607
He probably only realized later
how serious it was

354
00:41:24,690 --> 00:41:28,277
to barge into a foreign reporter's house
and try to make a woman undress

355
00:41:28,819 --> 00:41:30,112
in times like these.

356
00:41:36,535 --> 00:41:38,204
Songjeon has been killed.

357
00:41:41,499 --> 00:41:42,333
What?

358
00:41:44,919 --> 00:41:45,961
You mean…

359
00:41:46,921 --> 00:41:50,341
the one who went in as a cook?

360
00:41:50,966 --> 00:41:52,092
Yes.

361
00:41:54,178 --> 00:41:55,721
Sato Hideo killed him.

362
00:41:58,933 --> 00:42:00,267
I saw it myself.

363
00:42:01,852 --> 00:42:02,978
Sato Hideo

364
00:42:03,896 --> 00:42:05,898
murdered Songjeon.

365
00:42:20,287 --> 00:42:21,247
Oh no, Hideo.

366
00:42:22,206 --> 00:42:23,499
What happened?

367
00:42:24,041 --> 00:42:25,751
- We need to call a doctor.
- No.

368
00:42:26,377 --> 00:42:27,378
There's no need.

369
00:42:29,463 --> 00:42:30,339
Goodnight.

370
00:43:01,370 --> 00:43:04,456
The accomplice fled
with a gunshot wound to the shoulder.

371
00:43:05,082 --> 00:43:08,377
Go to every female guest's house
and check for gunshot wounds.

372
00:43:08,460 --> 00:43:09,378
- Yes, sir!
- Yes, sir!

373
00:43:09,461 --> 00:43:13,716
The suspect left one pearl earring
at the scene. Use it as a lead!

374
00:43:14,425 --> 00:43:15,426
- Yes, sir!
- Yes, sir!

375
00:43:20,598 --> 00:43:23,559
She's the wife of Yoo Philip,
an American correspondent from WWP News.

376
00:43:34,278 --> 00:43:35,154
Mr. Hideo.

377
00:43:44,163 --> 00:43:45,122
Hold on!

378
00:43:58,260 --> 00:44:00,596
Mr. Yoo is
an important foreign correspondent.

379
00:44:00,679 --> 00:44:02,723
Conduct the investigation carefully.

380
00:44:02,806 --> 00:44:03,849
If handled poorly,

381
00:44:03,932 --> 00:44:06,477
this could become a diplomatic issue.
That's why I came along.

382
00:44:06,560 --> 00:44:07,770
- Yes, sir.
- Yes, sir.

383
00:44:10,397 --> 00:44:11,357
This way.

384
00:44:18,113 --> 00:44:19,156
Mr. Sato.

385
00:44:19,948 --> 00:44:21,283
Under your foot…

386
00:44:24,745 --> 00:44:25,621
Like this?

387
00:44:39,134 --> 00:44:40,427
It can't be…

388
00:46:06,513 --> 00:46:08,307
<i>On the night of May 15th,</i>

389
00:46:08,390 --> 00:46:10,642
<i>the Gugukdan carried out a bomb attack</i>

390
00:46:10,726 --> 00:46:13,937
<i>at a pneumatic tube opening banquet</i>
<i>at the Government-General,</i>

391
00:46:14,646 --> 00:46:17,357
<i>resulting in heavy casualties</i>
<i>among Japanese officials.</i>

392
00:46:20,944 --> 00:46:24,782
<i>Comrade Songjeon, a Japanese member</i>
<i>of the Gugukdan who died in the attack,</i>

393
00:46:24,865 --> 00:46:26,408
<i>will not have sacrificed his life in vain.</i>

394
00:46:27,367 --> 00:46:29,787
<i>We will remember</i>
<i>our fallen comrade, Songjeon,</i>

395
00:46:31,163 --> 00:46:34,291
<i>and never stop fighting</i>
<i>for Joseon's independence.</i>

396
00:46:34,792 --> 00:46:35,626
<i>Joseon…</i>

397
00:46:36,835 --> 00:46:38,837
<i>will achieve independence, without fail.</i>

398
00:46:49,306 --> 00:46:51,391
Extra!

399
00:46:53,560 --> 00:46:55,938
Extra!

400
00:47:20,671 --> 00:47:23,549
GUGUKDAN CARRIES OUT BOMBING
AT GOVERNMENT-GENERAL

401
00:47:23,632 --> 00:47:24,800
It's in the paper.

402
00:47:25,843 --> 00:47:26,927
That's a relief.

403
00:47:28,220 --> 00:47:30,055
Did Suzuki tip them off?

404
00:47:31,807 --> 00:47:32,891
Myung-nam.

405
00:47:33,392 --> 00:47:35,394
Myung-nam, did you see this?

406
00:47:35,978 --> 00:47:36,854
What is it?

407
00:47:37,563 --> 00:47:40,274
Those lunatics.
Have they completely lost their minds?

408
00:47:40,357 --> 00:47:42,234
GOVERNMENT-GENERAL BOMBING
BY A DERANGED LONER

409
00:47:42,317 --> 00:47:44,403
How dare they cause a ruckus
in that hallowed place?

410
00:47:44,486 --> 00:47:45,988
They're utterly shameless.

411
00:47:46,530 --> 00:47:49,366
At least the culprit took his own life.

412
00:47:49,449 --> 00:47:50,617
It's a relief.

413
00:47:51,827 --> 00:47:52,995
Lunatics?

414
00:47:55,163 --> 00:47:56,164
Haven't you seen this?

415
00:47:57,165 --> 00:47:58,917
The Gugukdan was behind
yesterday's attack.

416
00:47:59,001 --> 00:48:00,752
The culprit wasn't crazy or suicidal--

417
00:48:00,836 --> 00:48:01,879
Enough!

418
00:48:01,962 --> 00:48:03,338
The Gugukdan?

419
00:48:05,090 --> 00:48:07,509
Don't even utter that disgraceful name.

420
00:48:08,093 --> 00:48:10,929
And don't read
seditious newspapers like this.

421
00:48:13,765 --> 00:48:16,602
Anyway, you know what, Myung-nam?

422
00:48:17,603 --> 00:48:20,397
Heaven was on my side.

423
00:48:22,858 --> 00:48:25,527
I had a stomachache,
so I was in the bathroom.

424
00:48:25,611 --> 00:48:28,822
I was upset about missing the toast
at the banquet.

425
00:48:29,406 --> 00:48:32,951
But thanks to that, I got away
from the bombing without a scratch.

426
00:48:33,035 --> 00:48:36,371
I'm telling you,
Heaven is looking out for me.

427
00:48:38,332 --> 00:48:39,625
I can't believe it.

428
00:48:41,043 --> 00:48:42,210
Have some coffee, Dad.

429
00:48:42,836 --> 00:48:44,087
All right.

430
00:48:47,633 --> 00:48:48,926
Oh my goodness.

431
00:48:49,009 --> 00:48:51,386
The coffee tastes extra sweet today.

432
00:48:51,470 --> 00:48:55,015
Nothing beats
the coffee my daughter makes.

433
00:48:58,977 --> 00:49:01,396
Sir, these documents need your signature.

434
00:49:06,151 --> 00:49:07,861
Ouch, my stomach.

435
00:49:08,654 --> 00:49:09,696
Hang on.

436
00:49:21,166 --> 00:49:22,292
The medicine in his drink

437
00:49:23,210 --> 00:49:25,545
at the banquet yesterday
must've been strong.

438
00:49:25,629 --> 00:49:26,505
No.

439
00:49:27,839 --> 00:49:30,217
He was saying things
I didn't want to hear this morning,

440
00:49:30,300 --> 00:49:31,468
so I put the rest in his coffee.

441
00:49:36,306 --> 00:49:37,140
Young-bok.

442
00:49:38,767 --> 00:49:42,020
If Kim Seung-eok, Joseon's
most notorious traitor, weren't my dad,

443
00:49:42,771 --> 00:49:46,108
would our comrades have suggested
using laxatives to protect him?

444
00:49:47,192 --> 00:49:48,026
Miss.

445
00:49:49,778 --> 00:49:51,029
I don't know anymore

446
00:49:51,989 --> 00:49:53,281
if I did the right thing.

447
00:49:55,575 --> 00:49:58,954
We're fighting to get rid of
pro-Japanese traitors for independence.

448
00:50:00,038 --> 00:50:02,249
So was it right to save my own father?

449
00:50:05,919 --> 00:50:08,338
How can he be so shameless?

450
00:50:09,381 --> 00:50:13,260
Songjeon is being humiliated like this.

451
00:50:30,527 --> 00:50:31,737
Pardon me.

452
00:50:59,389 --> 00:51:00,307
Sir,

453
00:51:01,892 --> 00:51:04,102
it seems the Gugukdan
carried out yesterday's attack.

454
00:51:04,186 --> 00:51:05,187
I saw it already.

455
00:51:06,563 --> 00:51:09,816
This morning, the governor-general called
me to his residence and yelled at me.

456
00:51:12,194 --> 00:51:13,236
I'm sorry.

457
00:51:13,320 --> 00:51:15,614
Joseon Central News says

458
00:51:15,697 --> 00:51:19,159
they published the story
based on an anonymous tip.

459
00:51:19,701 --> 00:51:21,244
But they claim they burned the original.

460
00:51:22,204 --> 00:51:23,663
I ordered the paper to be shut down.

461
00:51:23,747 --> 00:51:26,166
And none of the women had gunshot wounds?

462
00:51:29,503 --> 00:51:31,213
There were at least four women

463
00:51:32,005 --> 00:51:34,216
at the banquet

464
00:51:35,300 --> 00:51:36,968
wearing pearl earrings yesterday.

465
00:51:40,764 --> 00:51:44,518
And none of these four had gunshot wounds
or any other injuries?

466
00:51:44,601 --> 00:51:45,852
About that…

467
00:51:47,979 --> 00:51:50,690
There's one thing you need to confirm.

468
00:51:51,316 --> 00:51:52,150
What is it?

469
00:51:52,234 --> 00:51:54,486
It'd be better to hear from them directly.

470
00:51:55,612 --> 00:51:56,446
Come in!

471
00:52:04,371 --> 00:52:06,790
These officers went
to Yoo Philip's house last night.

472
00:52:09,960 --> 00:52:11,086
Who is it?

473
00:52:11,169 --> 00:52:13,088
We're from the Government-General.

474
00:52:15,423 --> 00:52:17,342
<i>We went with Interpreter Sato.</i>

475
00:52:18,260 --> 00:52:19,094
<i>With Hideo?</i>

476
00:52:19,177 --> 00:52:20,345
Correct, sir.

477
00:52:20,428 --> 00:52:23,098
Did you check the woman
for gunshot wounds?

478
00:52:23,181 --> 00:52:26,726
We checked a young woman
named Keum Seo-hee, and she was clear.

479
00:52:27,686 --> 00:52:29,437
<i>But the American reporter's wife…</i>

480
00:52:29,521 --> 00:52:30,772
we didn't check her.

481
00:52:30,856 --> 00:52:32,440
You didn't check her?

482
00:52:32,524 --> 00:52:34,526
That's right. The thing is…

483
00:52:35,193 --> 00:52:36,027
Let's get going.

484
00:52:36,111 --> 00:52:40,240
<i>Interpreter Sato said</i>
<i>we didn't need to check her.</i>

485
00:52:45,704 --> 00:52:47,205
All right. You may leave.

486
00:52:47,289 --> 00:52:48,290
- Yes, sir!
- Yes, sir!

487
00:52:53,420 --> 00:52:54,379
What should we do?

488
00:53:06,433 --> 00:53:09,561
Allow me to introduce my wife.
She's Chinese.

489
00:53:11,563 --> 00:53:12,814
What's going on?

490
00:53:14,608 --> 00:53:17,152
Mrs. Yoo's button came off.

491
00:53:23,241 --> 00:53:25,619
Find out whether anyone in Kyeam-ri

492
00:53:26,411 --> 00:53:27,579
knows this woman.

493
00:53:32,751 --> 00:53:35,754
Do you think she's Keum Seok-jung's wife?

494
00:53:49,309 --> 00:53:52,145
Since the Gugukdan was behind
yesterday's attack,

495
00:53:53,396 --> 00:53:55,565
could she be one of them too?

496
00:54:02,322 --> 00:54:03,156
Good morning.

497
00:54:06,868 --> 00:54:07,702
Who is it?

498
00:54:08,578 --> 00:54:09,746
It's Interpreter Sato.

499
00:54:16,127 --> 00:54:17,170
Oh, Hideo!

500
00:54:17,879 --> 00:54:18,797
Hello.

501
00:54:21,675 --> 00:54:22,509
Please come in.

502
00:54:23,093 --> 00:54:23,927
By the way,

503
00:54:24,010 --> 00:54:25,804
I heard about your engagement.

504
00:54:26,304 --> 00:54:27,305
Congratulations.

505
00:54:27,389 --> 00:54:28,723
Thanks.

506
00:54:46,157 --> 00:54:47,492
Soon you won't walk around

507
00:54:47,575 --> 00:54:51,246
with your head held high,
pretending to be Japanese.

508
00:54:53,206 --> 00:54:54,457
<i>Josenjing.</i>

509
00:55:03,133 --> 00:55:04,467
Your wound is pretty deep.

510
00:55:05,468 --> 00:55:07,262
Shouldn't you get it checked
at the hospital?

511
00:55:07,345 --> 00:55:08,263
Don't worry.

512
00:55:08,346 --> 00:55:11,516
There's no shortage of people
who want me dead.

513
00:55:12,892 --> 00:55:13,727
But…

514
00:55:15,061 --> 00:55:16,146
I'm not going to die.

515
00:55:46,509 --> 00:55:47,927
I acted rashly yesterday.

516
00:55:48,553 --> 00:55:51,931
I shouldn't have let my feelings
get the better of me. Forgive me.

517
00:55:52,015 --> 00:55:55,268
Should I be glad
you've finally realized it?

518
00:55:56,811 --> 00:55:57,645
I'm sorry.

519
00:56:03,777 --> 00:56:04,611
Apart from…

520
00:56:06,446 --> 00:56:08,406
finding out that the Gugukdan did it,

521
00:56:08,990 --> 00:56:10,492
did you find anything else?

522
00:56:18,041 --> 00:56:19,250
Why do you want to know?

523
00:56:19,334 --> 00:56:21,294
I'm worried about you, Father.

524
00:56:22,379 --> 00:56:23,671
Yesterday's attack was serious.

525
00:56:27,008 --> 00:56:30,678
Focus on the foreign press and prepare
for the League of Nations' visit.

526
00:56:32,263 --> 00:56:33,932
That's what you need to worry about,

527
00:56:35,100 --> 00:56:36,810
nothing else, Interpreter Sato.

528
00:56:39,104 --> 00:56:40,355
Yes, understood.

529
00:56:40,438 --> 00:56:43,983
The governor-general will be working
from his residence for the time being.

530
00:56:46,403 --> 00:56:47,237
Keep that in mind.

531
00:56:48,571 --> 00:56:49,823
Understood.

532
00:56:56,496 --> 00:56:58,915
Why did you go
to Yoo Philip's house last night?

533
00:57:10,885 --> 00:57:12,720
Given how important the foreign press is,

534
00:57:12,804 --> 00:57:15,432
I was worried the officers
might offend them, so I went along.

535
00:57:17,976 --> 00:57:19,978
Only Yoo Philip's story was in line

536
00:57:20,061 --> 00:57:22,313
with the official position
of the Government-General.

537
00:57:24,357 --> 00:57:25,233
He reported

538
00:57:25,900 --> 00:57:28,278
that a mentally unstable Japanese man
was behind the attack.

539
00:57:29,571 --> 00:57:31,531
You seem to be
getting along well with him.

540
00:57:32,657 --> 00:57:33,575
Fine. You may go.

541
00:57:34,701 --> 00:57:35,535
Yes, sir.

542
00:57:55,430 --> 00:57:58,766
ENGLISH TRANSLATION UNIT

543
00:58:04,230 --> 00:58:05,899
<i>I saw it myself.</i>

544
00:58:06,483 --> 00:58:07,609
Sato Hideo

545
00:58:08,526 --> 00:58:10,570
murdered Songjeon.

546
00:58:25,543 --> 00:58:27,003
Hello.

547
00:58:42,602 --> 00:58:43,436
Ms. Keum.

548
00:58:45,730 --> 00:58:46,564
Yes?

549
00:58:56,366 --> 00:58:57,200
What am I…

550
00:59:01,246 --> 00:59:03,164
supposed to call you?

551
00:59:05,124 --> 00:59:05,959
Sorry?

552
00:59:06,042 --> 00:59:07,252
Keum Seo-hee?

553
00:59:09,379 --> 00:59:10,296
Lee Gae-ttong?

554
00:59:12,131 --> 00:59:14,551
I'm not sure what you mean
out of the blue.

555
00:59:17,136 --> 00:59:18,012
So…

556
00:59:23,726 --> 00:59:25,395
what's your real name?

557
00:59:36,072 --> 00:59:36,906
You are…

558
00:59:39,784 --> 00:59:40,868
a spy, aren't you?

559
01:00:36,674 --> 01:00:40,595
100 DAYS OF DECEPTION

560
01:00:43,139 --> 01:00:44,015
<i>Her cover's been blown?</i>

561
01:00:44,474 --> 01:00:47,101
Isn't there a <i>Josenjing </i>who comes and goes
at the Government-General

562
01:00:47,185 --> 01:00:49,312
<i>every day,</i>
<i>riding on an American's coattails?</i>

563
01:00:49,395 --> 01:00:50,688
A pawn ticket?

564
01:00:50,772 --> 01:00:52,315
<i>Looks like I'm an unwelcome guest.</i>

565
01:00:52,899 --> 01:00:54,525
<i>But since I'm already here,</i>

566
01:00:54,609 --> 01:00:55,777
you may as well keep acting.

567
01:00:55,860 --> 01:00:59,030
<i>Then wouldn't it be</i>
<i>better to get rid of her?</i>

568
01:01:00,198 --> 01:01:01,240
Who are you people?

569
01:01:03,409 --> 01:01:04,994
<i>Do you believe people can change?</i>

570
01:01:05,078 --> 01:01:07,538
I think they can.

571
01:01:07,622 --> 01:01:08,831
Nice to meet you,

572
01:01:09,916 --> 01:01:10,958
Ms. Lee Ga-kyung.

573
01:01:17,674 --> 01:01:20,009
Subtitle translation by: Youji Kim
